# youtube-dl — real-world examples

Note: youtube-dl development has largely stalled — `yt-dlp` is the active
fork and accepts the same options shown here (on Tumbleweed:
`sudo zypper in yt-dlp`).

## Formats

```sh
# List all available formats for a video
youtube-dl -F example.com/watch?v=id

# Download format 22 (720p MP4)
youtube-dl -f 22 example.com/watch?v=id

# Try formats in preference order: 22, then 45, then 120
youtube-dl -f 22/45/120 example.com/watch?v=id

# yt-dlp-era approach: best video+audio up to 1080p
yt-dlp -f 'bestvideo[height<=1080]+bestaudio/best' URL
```

## Output templates

```sh
# Save as /$uploader/$date/$title.$ext
youtube-dl -o '%(uploader)s/%(date)s/%(title)s.%(ext)s' example.com/watch?v=id
```

## Playlists and simulation

```sh
# Download a playlist starting from video 5 (quote the & !)
youtube-dl --playlist-start 5 'example.com/watch?v=id&list=listid'

# Simulate — print what would happen without downloading
youtube-dl -s example.com/watch?v=id
```

## Homelab uses

```sh
# Archive a channel into the Plex library on mediaserver, skipping repeats
yt-dlp --download-archive /srv/media/yt/archive.txt \
  -o '/srv/media/yt/%(uploader)s/%(title)s.%(ext)s' <channel-url>

# Audio only, as mp3, for the car
yt-dlp -x --audio-format mp3 URL

# Grab subtitles too, embedded for Plex
yt-dlp --write-auto-subs --embed-subs URL
```

## Flag reference

| Flag | Meaning |
|------|---------|
| `-F` | list formats |
| `-f FMT` | pick format (`/` = fallback order) |
| `-o TMPL` | output filename template |
| `-s` | simulate only |
| `--playlist-start N` | start playlist at item N |
| `-x` | extract audio |
| `--download-archive F` | record/skip already-downloaded IDs |
